WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:06.060
Hey, accidents happen. But if you're in a&nbsp;
movie, sometimes accidents can turn out to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:11.760
be amazing. In fact, some of the most memorable&nbsp;
moments in cinema were actually the result of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.389
mistakes that the filmmakers decided were too&nbsp;
good to leave out of the finished movie. 
&nbsp;

00:00:16.389 --> 00:00:22.140
Alfred Hitchcock coined the term "MacGuffin"&nbsp;
to describe important objects upon which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.920
plot of a movie revolves — like&nbsp;
the briefcase in Pulp Fiction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.500
or the Infinity Stones in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe. As any comic fan will tell you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.640
the Infinity Stones are incredibly&nbsp;
important and powerful — which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.600
why it's so funny when Chris Pratt, as&nbsp;
Star-Lord, just straight up drops one.

00:00:48.234 --> 00:00:53.640
It looks totally scripted, but according to&nbsp;
the DVD commentary, that was an actual moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.680
of clumsiness from Pratt. But the moment&nbsp;
works, so director James Gunn left it in.
 &nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:05.580
As war looms and the loss of Rohan seems imminent,&nbsp;
Eowyn stares off into the distance as the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.460
reveals a wide scenic shot. As she does, a&nbsp;
flag on a pole whips in the wind. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:15.300
perfectly expressing the mood of&nbsp;
uncertainty and destruction to come,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:20.940
the flag rips off the pole and flies away.&nbsp;
The metaphor was not only beautifully apt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:26.649
but it was totally accidental. Director Peter&nbsp;
Jackson didn't plan for this — it was just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.649 --> 00:01:28.722
really windy on that day of shooting.

00:01:28.722 --> 00:01:31.602
This movie made Anne Hathaway a star, 

00:01:31.602 --> 00:01:38.280
and the DVD has become a slumber party classic for&nbsp;
little kids. It's a classic ugly duckling story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.940
of a girl named Mia who finds out she's the heir&nbsp;
apparent to a small European kingdom. But she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:50.640
so very awkward, y'see, falling down all the&nbsp;
time, as she learns how to carry herself with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.440
the grace and poise befitting a royal. At one&nbsp;
point, Mia is dancing on some bleachers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.900
the rain at her private school with her best&nbsp;
friend when she takes a pretty nasty tumble.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.320
That's no stuntwoman, and that&nbsp;
wasn't supposed to happen:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.160
Hathaway really did slip, falling&nbsp;
hard. But it fit in so well with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.738
the character's awkwardness that&nbsp;
it was kept in the finished film.
 &nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.030
The idea was simple: Bond-girl rides a horse&nbsp;
along the beach, and Daniel Craig swims up&nbsp;to her. 

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:28.440
But as he floated up, the actor ran&nbsp;
into a sand bank, giving him no choice but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.480
quickly stand up on his feet and stomp out of&nbsp;
the water. Suddenly, Bond is standing there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.220
muscled-up body on display, along&nbsp;
with some very revealing swim trunks.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:44.460
Casino Royale was a reboot of the Bond series,&nbsp;
so most fans assumed this was an homage to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:50.709
classic scene from the first Bond film, Dr. No,&nbsp;
where Ursula Andress emerges from the ocean.

00:02:50.709 --> 00:02:53.049
But it was actually just a lucky accident.

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:55.890
Indiana Jones wasn't the only raider of the 

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:02.220
lost ark in, uh, Raiders of the Lost Ark. There&nbsp;
was also evil French archaeologist Rene Belloq,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.094
who suffers a doubly horrible fate:&nbsp;
not only does his head explode…

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:17.100
...but he also eats a fly! Well, okay, not&nbsp;
exactly. During a closeup of actor Paul Freeman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:23.040
a fly appears to crawl right down Belloq's&nbsp;
throat. Freeman later revealed that the insect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.900
actually flew away after crawling around&nbsp;
his lips for a moment, but some judicious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:34.080
editing after the fact made it look like the&nbsp;
cold blooded Belloq just ate the fly instead.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.940
The police lineup scene early in the movie&nbsp;
is crucial to the plot of The Usual Suspects:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.400
it's where all the criminal main characters meet&nbsp;
to arrange a big heist. As each character steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:49.920
forward to read the line police told them to read,&nbsp;
none of them can contain their laughter, perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.440
setting up the dynamic between the characters as&nbsp;
well as their collective disregard for the cops.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.740
But the laughing wasn't in the script,&nbsp;
and it wasn't a directorial note. No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:10.200
the cast of The Usual Suspects couldn't&nbsp;
stop laughing because actor Benicio Del Toro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.620
couldn't stop farting. Co-star Kevin Pollak&nbsp;
says he farted on "like 12 takes in a row."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:23.400
Director Bryan Singer got so mad with the cast's&nbsp;
inability to focus that he yelled at them … which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:28.980
probably only made them laugh harder.
 
Speaking of farts, Rain Man is that rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:34.500
film that won the Oscar for best picture and also&nbsp;
included one of the most memorable fart scenes in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:40.896
movie history. Brothers Raymond and Charlie are&nbsp;
in a phone booth together, and Raymond passes gas.

00:04:41.235 --> 00:04:42.368
"Uh oh, fart."

00:04:42.368 --> 00:04:44.346
"Yeah, Charlie Babbitt, I'll hold."

00:04:44.347 --> 00:04:45.480
"Uh oh, fart."

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:46.320
"Did you fart Ray?"

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.458
"Fart."
"How can you stand that?"

00:04:48.458 --> 00:04:49.468
"I don't mind."

00:04:49.468 --> 00:04:56.280
That was real, and accidental. Stars Dustin&nbsp;
Hoffman and Tom Cruise both managed to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:01.456
completely in character, setting up a&nbsp;
classic moment in cinematic fart history.

00:05:01.456 --> 00:05:05.880
One of Django's chief aims in this&nbsp;
2012 Western is to free his wife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.680
from the clutches of terrifyingly crazed&nbsp;
slave owner Calvin Candie. The meeting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.420
between Jamie Foxx's Django and Leonardo&nbsp;
DiCaprio's Calvin is extremely tense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:20.255
and for dramatic effect, DiCaprio slams his&nbsp;
hand on a table … and right onto a glass.

00:05:20.255 --> 00:05:28.140
"Lay your palm flat on that&nbsp;
tabletop. If you lift those palms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:28.920
off that turtleshell tabletop, Mr Butch is gonna&nbsp;
let loose with both barrels of that sawed-off!"

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.660
DiCaprio didn't stop at all during the take,&nbsp;
even though he just seriously lacerated his hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.260
nonchalantly picking out the pieces of&nbsp;
glass and even lighting a cigarette.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:40.800
Daryl Hannah took home a souvenir&nbsp;
from the set of Blade Runner in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.040
form of a scar on her arm … and the&nbsp;
take in which she injured it making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.180
its way into the movie. While playing&nbsp;
a replicant named Pris, Hannah ran from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.628
William Sanderson's genetic designer J.F.&nbsp;
Sebastian and slipped on the wet pavement …

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:02.940
Breaking the window wasn't planned,&nbsp;
but Hannah finished filming the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:06.960
preserving what ended up being a&nbsp;
fairly serious injury for posterity.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:11.760
For her role as the manipulative outlaw Daisy in&nbsp;
Quentin Tarantino's The Hateful Eight, Jennifer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.380
Jason Leigh had to know how to play a little&nbsp;
guitar. She learned on a vintage 19th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:22.020
Martin, on loan to the production from the Martin&nbsp;
Guitar Museum. In one scene, Leigh's co-star Kurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.220
Russell was supposed to yank the instrument out&nbsp;
of her hands and smash it to smithereens—but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:30.382
the cameras were supposed to cut first, so it&nbsp;
could be swapped out for an ordinary guitar.

00:06:31.166 --> 00:06:32.580
"Music time's over."

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:35.220
"Whoa whoa whoa!"

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:40.140
But the cut didn't come, and Russell&nbsp;
unknowingly destroyed the antique Martin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:44.280
valued at $40,000—so Leigh's&nbsp;
reaction is the real thing.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.320
"Whoa whoa whoa!"

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:52.440
James Cameron's 1994 spy comedy contains some&nbsp;
of the biggest and most expensive sequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.580
in movie history, but the one scene from&nbsp;
True Lies everybody remembers two decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:01.680
later is Jamie Lee Curtis's striptease. Her&nbsp;
character, Helen, takes off her clothes for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.820
man she believes to be an arms dealer, but&nbsp;
is actually her husband, Harry, played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:11.680
Arnold Schwarzenegger. Curtis's sexy moment is&nbsp;
interrupted briefly when she loses her balance …

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.860
It's a perfect pratfall with impeccable&nbsp;
comic timing, but it wasn't in Cameron's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:23.460
script. Curtis didn't want to do the scene&nbsp;
the way it was scripted, which called for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:27.720
her to get completely naked and be filmed in&nbsp;
silhouette. Cameron agreed to her changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.100
and they worked out the choreography without&nbsp;
Schwarzenegger's knowledge. So that's a genuine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:36.540
reaction from Schwarzenegger, who thought the&nbsp;
fall would ultimately just end up as a blooper.

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.580
The 1969 low budget classic Midnight Cowboy&nbsp;
features one of the most famous unscripted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:47.100
lines in movie history. As Dustin Hoffman's Ratso&nbsp;
Rizzo and Jon Voight's Joe Buck are walking across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.700
a very busy and very real Manhattan street having&nbsp;
a conversation, a cab nearly hits Hoffman ...

00:07:53.929 --> 00:07:57.563
"Hey, I'm walking here! I'm walking here!"

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:02.460
Hoffman's response was so perfectly in&nbsp;
character that director John Schlesinger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.120
kept it in the film, which went on to&nbsp;
win Best Picture at the Academy Awards.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:11.880
It's a scene originally meant to serve as just a&nbsp;
transition allowing Woody Allen's proud New Yorker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.380
character Alvy and his girlfriend Annie to mention&nbsp;
they're taking a trip to Los Angeles — but it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:21.000
become a classic bit of physical comedy, thanks&nbsp;
to an unplanned moment. A stash of cocaine said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:27.280
to be worth $2,000 an ounce is passed around&nbsp;
at a party, eventually arriving at Alvy ...

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.980
"And what is the kick of it, I never … ACHOO!"

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:36.060
Allen says it was "a complete, unplanned&nbsp;
accident," but test screening audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.060 --> 00:08:40.260
laughed uproariously at it, so Allen, also&nbsp;
the director of the movie, kept it in.

